--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The darkness shivers, and a single rectangle of light blossoms before you like a doorway hung in nothing. Through it glides the first card, turning as it comes, until the face of The High Priestess fills your vision—moon-crowned, scroll in hand, seated between twin pillars of night and day. A hush of recognition passes through you, as if a cool hand has been laid across your brow; the card’s surface is slick under your fingertips though you have not moved, and you taste parchment and altar incense that were never there.

A second card flips from beneath the first, and now the Two of Wands lies exposed: a figure stands on a battlement of sun-baked stone, the world cradled in one palm, the other gripping a living staff that smells faintly of cedar sap. Your own palms prickle with the ghost of that wood, and for an instant you feel the wind of every open horizon that waits beyond the walls of whatever life you have known.

The third card spirals up between them, landing face-up with a soft snap. The Six of Swords shows a ferryman poling a narrow boat across slate-gray water; six blades hang overhead like a silent constellation. The oar’s drip of river water seems to echo inside your ribs, and you understand—without language—that every edge you see is also a path, every wound a map.

These cards are now yours. They hum in your memory.

The rectangle folds inward, swallowing the images, and the void peels away like burnt paper. Cold stone presses against your back; the air is thick with sour ale, tallow smoke, and the iron tang of old blood. You are lying on the floor of the Gilded Griffon taproom in Aurelion’s river quarter, dawn light just beginning to crawl through the shutters while the city beyond stirs toward another day of kingless uncertainty.

