--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
Callum’s knuckles whiten as he grips the rim of the well’s mossy coping, the morning light catching the sweat on his brow. “Fifty it is, then,” he mutters, voice low enough that the pigeons on the well-head stop pecking to listen. He reaches inside his patched wool doublet and produces a worn leather pouch; the coins clink like distant hail as he counts them into his palm, each one scraped and dulled from years of taverns and toll-roads. “But understand this: those samples are my season’s credit with the Aurelion guildhouse. If you vanish, I don’t just lose goods—I lose standing.” His eyes flick to the forest edge visible beyond the square’s last stone house, where the pines already lean together like gossiping crones.

To bind the bargain, he unhooks a thin brass ring from his belt and presses it into your hand; the metal is still warm from his skin, its inner rim etched with the tiny scales of the Solis merchant mark. “Show that to any trader on the Valmere road and they’ll know I vouched for you,” he says, voice steadier now that the pledge is physical. Then, almost as an afterthought, he pulls a strip of indigo cloth from the same pocket and ties it round the ring. “Tuck this in the lockbox latch when you bring it back—proof it’s untouched. If the cloth’s missing, our deal’s void.” With that he steps aside, clearing the path between you and the whispering dark of the trees, the pouch of fifty gold heavy in his waiting hand.

