The morning sun glints off the worn cobbles of Old Well Square, catching the nervous sweat on Callum’s brow as he paces a tight circle around the mossy lip of the well. His linen shirt is dark under the arms, and every few steps he stops to wring his calloused hands, glancing toward the eastern archway as though the answer to his trouble might stride in on its own. When he spots you, relief flickers across his weather-creased face; he closes the distance quickly, boots scuffing over loose grit.

“Please—wait a moment,” he says, voice low so the gossiping fishwives by the fountain won’t overhear. “My cart was ransacked last night while I slept in the loft. They took nearly everything, but the strangest part: the thieves left my lockbox sitting out at the Whispering Forest Edge, like bait. Nothing deadly in it, just trade samples—dyes, spice packets, a few silverleaf seeds—yet without them I can’t prove quality to tomorrow’s buyers.” He wipes his forehead with a neckerchief that smells of anise and worry. “I’d fetch it myself, but I’ve orders to fill before dusk, and the forest path… well, it feels wrong today. If you’re heading that way, could you bring it back? I’ll stand a drink at the Hearthstone once market closes.” His gaze flickers to your satchel, silently measuring whether you’re the sort willing to help a stranger.

