The village of Virell falls behind you like a sigh. Cobbles give way to hard-packed earth, then to needle-carpeted trail that winds between the first outriders of the Whispering Forest. Hammer-song and oven-smoke fade, replaced by the hush of resinous pines and the slow drip of last night’s rain from glossy holly leaves. Each footfall crunches louder than it should, as though the path narrows to hush every noise you make.

Ten paces farther, the trail kinks around a fallen yew whose bark has gone black and slick with age. Half-tucked beneath its upturned roots and a net of last year’s bracken lies an iron-banded lockbox, no larger than a loaf, its hinges furred with orange rust. Before you close the distance, a shadow twitches against the far side of the log—too low for wind, too deliberate for falling twigs. The undergrowth shifts again with a soft, deliberate scrape, and the smell of bruised moss drifts toward you like a warning.

