Brambles lace the narrowing path, tugging at your sleeves while trunks press closer, their bark slick with moss and winter damp. Ten paces ahead, a fallen log—its heartwood silvered by rot—lies half across the trail. Wedged beneath its upturned roots glints a corner of iron: a palm-sized lockbox, ivy stitching it to the earth like nature’s own sutures. Before you can close the distance, a twig snaps to your left, too heavy for wind. Something shifts within the undergrowth, black on black, breathing in time with the creak of the forest. The box waits; the shadow watches.

